### TL;DR

A square radiant cut diamond is a radiant cut with a length-to-width ratio between 0.95 and 1.05. It has 70 brilliant-cut facets, clipped corners, and the same fire and sparkle as its elongated counterpart — in a compact, symmetrical silhouette.

**The core insight:** The square radiant sits in the middle of the most important shape decision for square diamond buyers. **The Princess Dilemma** — do you buy a princess cut (cheaper, sharper corners, higher chip risk) or a square radiant (more expensive, safer corners, more facets)? This post answers that question with data.

**The Square Threshold:** Any radiant cut with an L/W ratio of 0.95–1.05 is a square radiant. Below 0.95 doesn't exist in practice. Above 1.10, it starts reading as rectangular. The certificate measurement is the only truth — not the listing name.

→ Start here: [1ct natural G-VS2 square radiant at $2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) + [Knife Edge Solitaire White Gold at $925](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/knife-edge-solitaire-by-james-allen-item-316048?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) = **$3,243 total ring**. Lab-grown: [1ct E-VVS1 at $867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) + setting = **$1,792 total ring**.

**Priority:** Check the L/W ratio on the GIA certificate. A ratio of 1.15 is rectangular, not square. Target 0.95–1.05. Everything else — clarity, color, setting — comes after that number.

---

## What Is a Square Radiant Cut Diamond?

A square radiant cut diamond is a radiant cut with a length-to-width (L/W) ratio between 0.95 and 1.05. It has four sides of roughly equal length, four clipped corners creating an octagonal silhouette, and 70 brilliant-cut facets across the crown and pavilion.

The square radiant is not a separate shape from the radiant cut. It is a radiant cut in its most compact, symmetrical form. The same 70-facet architecture that gives the elongated radiant its fire also gives the square radiant its sparkle — just in a square silhouette rather than a rectangular one.

I'm Farzana Hasan, GIA Expert at [Diamond Critics](/about-farzana/). The square radiant is one of the most misunderstood shapes in the market. Buyers search for it expecting a different shape from princess cut but aren't always sure why they want it. This guide gives you the exact data to make the right call. For the full radiant family overview, see **[Radiant Cut Diamond: The Complete Buying Guide](/radiant-cut-diamond)**.

---

## The Square Threshold

![Square radiant cut diamond L/W ratio guide — 0.95 to 1.05 square zone vs rectangular radiant zone](/images/square-radiant-cut-diamond-lw-ratio-guide.avif)

The Square Threshold is the L/W ratio range of 0.95 to 1.05 that defines a true square radiant cut. Outside this window, the stone is either not a radiant cut at all or slides into rectangular territory.

Here is the full ratio map for radiant cuts:

| L/W Ratio | Classification | What You See |
|---|---|---|
| 0.95–1.05 | **Square radiant (target zone)** | Nearly equal sides — compact and symmetrical |
| 1.06–1.19 | Tweener radiant | Slightly rectangular — neither clearly square nor elongated |
| 1.20–1.35 | Elongated radiant | Visibly longer — finger-flattering rectangular silhouette |
| 1.36–1.45 | Extended elongated | Narrow and long |
| 1.46+ | Too elongated | Can appear thin or fragile |

**The sweet spot inside the square zone is 1.00–1.03.** At exactly 1.00, the stone is perfectly square. At 1.03, it's still visually square to any eye. Above 1.07, the stone reads as slightly rectangular — not what most square radiant buyers are picturing.

**Where to find the ratio:** Your GIA certificate lists measurements as "L × W × D" in millimeters. Divide length by width. A stone that measures 6.5mm × 6.2mm has an L/W ratio of 1.05 — still in the square zone. A stone that measures 6.8mm × 5.9mm has an L/W ratio of 1.15 — that is a rectangular radiant, not a square one.

**What sellers don't tell you:** "Square radiant" is a retailer term, not a GIA category. Any radiant with a ratio under 1.10 might be called square on a product listing. Always divide the certificate measurements yourself. Never rely on the label.

---

## The Princess Dilemma

The Princess Dilemma is the decision every square diamond buyer faces: square radiant or princess cut? Both are square. Both are brilliant-cut. Both are modern and geometric. The differences are structural, not cosmetic.

**What princess cuts have over square radiants:**

Princess cuts are generally 15–25% cheaper than square radiant cuts at equivalent carat, color, and clarity. If budget is the primary factor, that matters. Princess cuts also have a sharper, more geometric outline because their corners are not clipped.

**What square radiants have over princess cuts:**

The square radiant has 70 facets versus the princess cut's 50–58. More facets mean more scintillation — more small flashes of light as the ring moves. The square radiant also has clipped corners, which eliminates the single biggest durability concern with princess cuts: corner chipping.

Princess cut corners are sharp 90-degree points. In daily wear, they can chip on surfaces, bag clasps, or during vigorous activity. The square radiant's clipped corners create 45-degree bevels — still geometric, but far more resistant to damage. Replacing a chipped corner on a princess cut is expensive and sometimes impossible without re-cutting the stone.

**The honest comparison:**

Buy a princess cut if you want the sharpest, most geometric outline available and your budget is the priority. Buy a square radiant if you want more facets, safer corners, and are willing to pay a modest premium for structural superiority.

Neither choice is wrong. This is a genuine trade-off. But most buyers who have owned both choose the square radiant for daily wear.

> "I've seen buyers come back after a princess cut chips its corner — sometimes within the first year of wearing. It's not rare. The square radiant's clipped corners solve that problem completely while keeping the same square silhouette. The premium over princess is usually $200–$500 on a 1ct stone. For a ring you'll wear every day for decades, that is the right trade." — Farzana Hasan, **[Diamond Critics](/about-farzana/)**

