## Square Radiant vs Princess Cut: The Full Comparison

![Square radiant cut vs princess cut diamond — face-up silhouette, corner safety, facet count, and certified price comparison](/images/square-radiant-vs-princess-cut-comparison.avif)

This is the comparison most buyers are researching when they search "square radiant cut diamond." Here is the full breakdown.

**Silhouette.** Both are square when viewed face-up. The princess cut has four sharp, 90-degree corners. The square radiant has four clipped corners creating an octagonal outline — still clearly square, but without the exposed sharp points. From two feet away, they look nearly identical. In a setting, the prong placement reveals the difference.

**Facets and fire.** Princess cuts use 50–58 facets depending on the specific cut style. Square radiant cuts use 70 facets. At equal carat and quality, the square radiant throws more scintillation — more individual flashes of light. It is a measurable optical difference, not a marketing claim.

**Corner chip risk.** This is the most important practical difference. Princess cut corners are sharp. In daily wear, they contact surfaces. A hard knock on a counter edge, a bag strap, or a gym floor can chip a corner — a damage type that often requires re-cutting the stone or replacing it. Square radiant corners are clipped at 45 degrees. They are protected. This is why jewelers recommend square radiants for people who are physically active or work with their hands.

**Price.** Princess cuts generally run 15–25% below square radiant cuts at equivalent specs. A 1ct princess G-VS2 might price at $1,800–$2,200, while a 1ct square radiant G-VS2 prices at [$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). That gap is real but shrinks when you factor in potential corner repair costs over a ring's lifetime.

**Cut grade.** GIA issues cut grades for princess cuts (Excellent, Very Good, Good). GIA does not issue cut grades for radiant cuts — including square radiants. "Ideal" or "Excellent Cut" labels on square radiant listings are retailer assessments, not lab grades. For princess cuts, stick to GIA Excellent. For square radiants, verify proportions manually from the certificate.

See also: **[Princess Cut Diamond: Complete Buying Guide](/princess-cut-diamond)**

