## Clarity Rules for Square Radiant Cut

**The floor is VS2.** The 70-facet brilliant-cut architecture is the most forgiving inclusion environment in square diamond cutting. A VS2 square radiant will appear completely eye-clean in normal lighting conditions.

**SI1 is possible, not guaranteed.** Some SI1 square radiant cuts are eye-clean — the 70 facets scatter light across a wide surface area and can mask small inclusions. But SI1 must be verified stone-by-stone through a video inspection or in person. Never buy SI1 sight-unseen from a photo.

**VVS and above is wasted money in a square radiant.** The 70-facet pattern makes VVS, VS1, and VS2 indistinguishable face-up in normal lighting. That budget goes further as carat weight or color grade.

**Where inclusions matter most in a square shape.** The center of the table is the most exposed zone. Inclusions in the corners are partially covered by prongs and therefore less visible. A VS2 with a corner inclusion under the prong is a better buy than a VS1 with a center-table feather. Always review the inclusion map on the certificate before purchasing.

---

## Color Guide for Square Radiant Cut

**Natural diamonds in white gold or platinum:** H is the floor. G-H is the sweet spot — eye-white in normal lighting without paying the D-F premium. Below H, body color can accumulate in the corners and along the edges of the square stone, especially visible in white metal settings.

**Natural diamonds in yellow or rose gold:** Go as low as J. The warm metal absorbs body color from the stone — an I or J color looks completely natural. A J-VS2 square radiant in yellow gold saves approximately 20–25% over a G-VS2 with no visible quality difference.

**Lab-grown in any metal:** Buy F or better. Lab-grown prices in the D-F color range are compressed — D-VVS1 lab radiant stones cost only slightly more than F-VVS1 in most cases. There is no reason to settle for H or I color in a lab stone.

**The corner color trap.** The square shape has four corners that sit at the stone's perimeter. In brilliant-cut diamonds, body color accumulates at the perimeter — corners and edges — before it becomes visible through the table center. This means a square radiant shows color earlier along its edges than a round brilliant of the same grade. The practical result: drop one color grade below what you would accept in a round cut. A G or H grade in a round might pass as colorless face-up; in a square radiant, the edges can show slight warmth at H. Stick to G in white metal settings unless you have reviewed the specific stone on video.

