## The 5 Rules for Buying a Square Radiant Cut Diamond

![Square radiant cut diamond buying checklist — L/W ratio target, VS2 clarity, H color, corner check, setting type](/images/square-radiant-cut-buying-checklist.avif)

Five rules. Apply all of them before you buy.

**Rule 1: Confirm L/W ratio is 0.95–1.05 on the GIA certificate.** This is the only definition of "square" that matters. Do not trust the listing label. A ratio of 1.12 is rectangular. Divide the length by the width on the certificate yourself.

**Rule 2: Buy VS2 clarity minimum.** The 70-facet pattern masks inclusions better than princess or cushion cuts. VS2 will be eye-clean. VVS is unnecessary — that money is better used on carat or color.

**Rule 3: Natural = H color minimum. Lab = F color minimum.** Below H in natural, body color collects in the corners of the square stone. In lab-grown, F-D is price-compressed — there is no reason to settle below F.

**Rule 4: Choose a four-prong square setting head.** The setting head must match the stone's square geometry. A round basket head fights the stone. Confirm millimeter dimensions of the setting head before ordering.

**Rule 5: Verify the corner prong placement.** In a four-prong setting, each prong should sit at a clipped corner of the stone. This both protects the corner and shows the stone's full square silhouette. Ask the jeweler to confirm prong-to-corner alignment if ordering remotely.

---

## Expert Summary

The square radiant cut is the best square brilliant diamond for buyers who want the fire of a radiant cut in a compact, symmetrical shape — and who want safer corners than a princess cut without paying the premium of a square cushion.

The shape's weaknesses are limited: it costs 15–25% more than a comparable princess cut, and GIA does not issue a cut grade for it. Both are manageable. The extra cost buys corner safety that matters over decades of daily wear. The missing GIA cut grade requires manual proportion verification from the certificate — a one-minute step.

At every carat level, the lab-grown version offers the same 70-facet architecture, the same L/W ratio, and the same certified quality at 60–88% below natural prices.

> "The square radiant cut is what I recommend when buyers come to me wanting a princess cut but are worried about chipping. The shape solves that problem completely. The 70 facets give more fire than princess. The clipped corners are safer. The only reason not to buy it is if you specifically want the sharpest corner outline that only princess cut delivers. If corners aren't the aesthetic priority, the square radiant wins on every other metric." — Farzana Hasan, **[Diamond Critics](/about-farzana/)**

---

## Final Verdict

**If you're choosing between square radiant and princess cut:** Buy princess if budget is your primary constraint and you accept the corner chip risk. Buy square radiant if you want more facets, clipped corners, and a ring that survives daily wear without corner damage. The premium is typically [$200–$500](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) on a 1ct stone — worth it for most buyers who plan to wear the ring daily.

**If you want a square radiant under $2,000 total:** A [1ct lab E-VVS1 at $867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) plus the [Knife Edge Solitaire at $925](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/knife-edge-solitaire-by-james-allen-item-316048?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) = a [$1,792](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/knife-edge-solitaire-by-james-allen-item-316048?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) certified diamond ring. Better than any CZ option at a fraction of the natural diamond price.

**If you're comparing square radiant vs CZ:** Lab-grown at [$1,792](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/knife-edge-solitaire-by-james-allen-item-316048?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is a real, certified, permanent diamond. CZ clouds within years of daily wear. If the budget can reach $1,800, lab-grown is the only rational choice.

**If your budget is $5,000+ for natural:** A [1.5ct G-VS2 square radiant at $7,704](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29651855?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the sweet spot — enough carat weight to command presence, G-VS2 specs that deliver eye-clean quality, and a setting budget of [$925](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/knife-edge-solitaire-by-james-allen-item-316048?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)–[$1,500](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/riviera-pave-item-195326?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) that doesn't compete with the stone.

