## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is a square radiant cut diamond?

A square radiant cut diamond is a radiant cut with a length-to-width ratio between 0.95 and 1.05. It has 70 brilliant-cut facets, four nearly equal sides, and four clipped corners creating an octagonal outline when viewed face-up. It is not a separate shape from the radiant cut — it is a radiant cut in its most compact, symmetrical form.

### What L/W ratio makes a radiant cut square?

A radiant cut is considered square when its L/W ratio is between 0.95 and 1.05. Above 1.10, it reads as rectangular or elongated. The ratio is calculated from the GIA certificate measurements — divide the length by the width. A stone listed as "square radiant" might have any ratio between 0.95 and 1.12 depending on the retailer's definition, so always verify the number yourself.

### Is a square radiant the same as a princess cut?

No. Both are square brilliant-cut diamonds, but they are structurally different. Princess cuts have four sharp 90-degree corners and 50–58 facets. Square radiant cuts have four clipped corners (creating an octagonal outline) and 70 facets. Princess cuts have a GIA cut grade; square radiant cuts do not. Princess cuts are generally 15–25% cheaper but carry a higher corner chip risk.

### Does the square radiant cut have GIA cut grade?

No. GIA does not issue cut grades for any radiant cut diamonds — square or elongated. "Ideal Cut" or "Excellent Cut" labels on square radiant listings are retailer assessments, not GIA grades. Always verify proportions manually from the GIA certificate before buying.

### What clarity do I need for a square radiant cut?

VS2 is the minimum. The 70-facet brilliant-cut pattern masks inclusions far better than princess or cushion cuts of the same clarity grade. A VS2 square radiant will appear eye-clean in normal lighting. SI1 can be eye-clean but must be verified on a stone-by-stone basis. VVS and above provides no visible benefit.

### Is a square radiant better than a princess cut?

For daily wear, yes — in most cases. The square radiant has more facets (70 vs 50–58), clipped corners that eliminate chip risk, and better inclusion hiding. Princess cuts are cheaper (15–25% typically) and have a sharper corner outline. If budget is the priority, princess wins. If durability and fire are the priority, square radiant wins.

### What is radiant cut CZ?

A radiant cut cubic zirconia (CZ) is a synthetic crystalline material cut in the radiant shape. CZ is dramatically cheaper than diamond — a 1ct CZ stone costs $5–$30. However, CZ has a Mohs hardness of 8–8.5 versus diamond's 10. In daily ring wear, CZ scratches and clouds within 1–3 years and must eventually be replaced. CZ carries no gemological certification. Lab-grown radiant diamonds start at [$867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for 1ct E-VVS1 — a real, certified, permanent diamond.

### How much does a square radiant cut diamond ring cost?

Entry point for a natural ring: [$3,243](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/knife-edge-solitaire-by-james-allen-item-316048?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — 1ct G-VS2 stone at [$2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) plus the Knife Edge Solitaire at [$925](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/knife-edge-solitaire-by-james-allen-item-316048?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Lab-grown entry: [$1,792](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/knife-edge-solitaire-by-james-allen-item-316048?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — 1ct E-VVS1 lab at [$867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) plus the same setting.

### What setting works best for a square radiant cut?

A four-prong setting with a square head — prongs placed at each clipped corner. This protects the corners and frames the square silhouette correctly. The [Knife Edge Solitaire at $925](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/knife-edge-solitaire-by-james-allen-item-316048?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and the [Riviera Pavé at $1,500](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/riviera-pave-item-195326?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) are the two most practical options. Avoid round basket settings — they conflict with the square geometry.

### What is the difference between square radiant and elongated radiant?

L/W ratio. A square radiant has an L/W ratio of 0.95–1.05 — nearly equal length and width, compact and symmetrical. An elongated radiant has an L/W ratio of 1.20–1.40 — visibly longer than wide, finger-elongating silhouette. Both have 70 facets and the same brilliant-cut architecture. The shape is different; the fire is the same.

### Is radiant cut CZ worth buying over lab-grown?

No — if you can reach a lab-grown budget. A [1ct lab E-VVS1 square radiant at $867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is a certified real diamond with Mohs hardness 10 that will not scratch, cloud, or degrade. CZ clouds within years of daily wear and has no certification. The total lab-grown ring is [$1,792](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/knife-edge-solitaire-by-james-allen-item-316048?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). A quality CZ ring in the same setting might run $400–$700. If the budget gap is the issue, lab-grown is worth stretching for.

### What color grade should I buy for a square radiant cut?

For natural diamonds in white metal: H minimum, G-H ideal. Below H, body color shows in the corners and edges of the square silhouette. For yellow or rose gold settings: I or J works well — the warm metal absorbs the color. For lab-grown: F or better, since D-F pricing is compressed in the lab market.

### How does square radiant compare to Asscher cut?

Both are square, both have clipped corners. The Asscher is step-cut (72 facets producing a hall-of-mirrors effect) and requires VS1 clarity because inclusions are visible through step facets. The square radiant is brilliant-cut (70 facets producing maximum fire) and works at VS2. Square radiant beats Asscher on clarity budget and light return. Asscher beats square radiant on the step-cut aesthetic — if you want the hall-of-mirrors look, buy Asscher.

